1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,540
You can’t cross the same river twice, the popular saying goes.
有句俗语说，人不能两次踏入同一条河流。

2
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:15,160
This wisdom reminds us that everything changes - expecting to relive your bygone days of success in the same way is unrealistic.
这句至理名言提醒我们，万事万物都在变化——想要以同样的方式重温往日的成功岁月是不切实际的。

3
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:20,220
But, apparently, this doesn’t apply to music.
但显然，这个道理并不适用于音乐领域。

4
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:31,940
Over the last decade or so, for one reason or another, a number of high-profile bands have decided to stop resting on their laurels and have reunited.
在过去十年左右的时间里，不知出于何种原因，许多知名乐队决定不再满足于过去的成就，纷纷选择重组。

5
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:34,170
But why?
那么，他们重组的原因究竟是什么呢？

6
00:00:34,170 --> 00:00:41,380
Earlier this year, rock band Genesis confirmed that they would be embarking on one more tour.
今年年初，摇滚乐队创世纪确认他们将再度开启巡演。

7
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:48,920
In November 2019, the Pussycat Dolls announced a slew of tour dates for April 2020.
2019年11月，小野猫组合宣布了2020年4月的一系列巡演日程。

8
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,670
In 2018, it was the Spice Girls’ comeback.
2018年，则是辣妹组合的回归之旅。

9
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:54,770
The list goes on.
类似的例子不胜枚举。

10
00:00:54,770 --> 00:00:59,560
Many bands have found new success in their resurrected form.
许多乐队重组后再创辉煌。

11
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:16,280
American alternative rock band The Pixies originally broke up in 1993 after playing for seven years. After an eleven-year hiatus, the band reunited in 2004 and have not stopped.
美国另类摇滚乐队小妖精在成立七年后于1993年解散，经过十一年的沉寂，乐队于2004年重组，此后便一直活跃在乐坛。

12
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:21,870
In some cases, it’s financial reasons that made them go back on the road.
在某些情况下，经济因素是促使他们重返舞台的原因。

13
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:30,720
“Touring these days is far more lucrative than it used to be,” according to Kara Weisenstein of online publisher Mic.
在线媒体Mic的卡拉·韦森斯坦表示：“如今的巡演比以往利润丰厚得多。”

14
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:40,290
This is because ticket prices have risen to compensate for the piracy that weakened music sales in the 90s, according to online publisher Bloomberg.
据在线媒体彭博社报道，这是因为唱片盗版现象曾在20世纪90年代重创音乐销量，而如今演唱会票价的上涨正是对这一损失的弥补。

15
00:01:40,290 --> 00:01:48,880
‘People are really into nostalgia right now,’ Lance Bass, member of former band NSYNC told Variety magazine.
前超级男孩组合成员兰斯·巴斯在接受《综艺》杂志采访时表示：“现在的人们特别热衷于怀旧。”

16
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:54,710
When The Pixies reformed, their 2004 comeback tour sold out within minutes.
小妖精乐队重组后，2004年的回归巡演门票几分钟内便销售一空。

17
00:01:54,710 --> 00:02:01,770
Is it possible that those teenage fans of yore have grown into nostalgic adults wistful for their youth?
难道当年那些十几岁的年轻粉丝，如今都已长成怀念青春、渴望重温旧梦的成年人了吗？

18
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:09,260
Bass thinks so. ‘I think fans just want to feel that innocence and excitement again,’ he says.
巴斯对此深信不疑，他说：“我觉得粉丝们只是想再次体验那种纯真和激动的感觉。”

19
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:13,180
But not everybody thinks that these reunions are a good thing.
但并非所有人都认为乐队重组是件好事。

20
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:20,370
“There’s something noble in not trying to go after past glories,” says NME writer Leonie Cooper.
英国音乐杂志《新音乐快递》的撰稿人莱奥妮·库珀表示：“不追逐昔日辉煌，本身就是一种高尚的做法。”

21
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:30,370
‘As much as I love ABBA… the fact they refused to risk putting on a show that might not match the excellence of their heyday is pretty impressive.’
“我虽然非常喜欢阿巴合唱团……但他们拒绝冒险举办可能无法重现全盛时期水准的演出，这一点着实令人敬佩。”

22
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:32,860
Should bands reunite?
乐队到底该不该重组？

23
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:34,670
Opinion is divided.
人们对此意见不一。

24
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:41,480
If things don’t go well, they run the risk of being labelled has-beens and tarnishing their reputation.
如果重组后的表现不尽如人意，乐队就有可能被贴上“过气明星”的标签，声誉也会受损。

25
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,800
But if their sound is as good as it ever was and the fans are happy, then who’s going to complain?
但如果他们的音乐水准依然在线，粉丝们也为之欢欣雀跃，那么又有谁会抱怨呢？

26
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:52,540
After all, you can never have too much of a good thing.
毕竟，好东西永远不嫌多。
